Below is a quick‑start guide that shows exactly **how** to create a new `ChartSeries` with custom axis labels, data points, and a default colour, and then drop that series straight into an existing chart layout (`chartLayoutObject`).  
Everything is written so you can copy/paste directly into a Node.js / browser environment that has access to the `functions.ChartSeriesGenerator` helper exposed by the system.

---

### 1️⃣ Prepare Your Data & Layout Objects  

| Variable | Type | Example |
|----------|------|---------|
| `axisLabelsArray` | Array\<String\> | `[“January”, “February”, “March”]` |
| `dataPointsArray` | Array\<Number\> | `[12, 25, 9]` |
| `defaultColor`     | String (hex/RGB/etc.) | `"#3498db"` |
| `chartLayoutObject` | Object | `{ title:"Monthly Sales", xAxis:{}, yAxis:{} }` |

> ⚠️ *Remember*: When passing these structures to the generator, each one needs to be serialized to a **string** because the API signature requires `"string"` parameters. That means wrapping the literal value in quotes and escaping internal double quotes when necessary.

```js
// 1️⃣ Define raw JavaScript objects first
const axisLabelsArray      = ["January","February","March"];
const dataPointsArray      = [12,25,9];
const defaultColor         = "#3498db";
const chartLayoutObject    = {
  title : "Monthly Sales",
  xAxis : { label: "Month" },
  yAxis : { label: "Units Sold" }
};
```

---

### 2️⃣ Call the Generator Function  

Now serialize everything to strings and invoke the helper:

```js
import { functions } from "./path/to/functions"; // adjust import path accordingly

async function addSeriesToChart() {
  const result = await functions.ChartSeriesGenerator({
    labels: JSON.stringify(axisLabelsArray),          // → "["January","February","March"]"
    data  : JSON.stringify(dataPointsArray),           // → "[12,25,9]"
    color : defaultColor,                             // optional – no serialization needed
    chartLayout: JSON.stringify(chartLayoutObject)     // → "{\"title\":\"Monthly Sales\",\"xAxis\":{\"label\":\"Month\"},…}"
  });

  console.log("New Series created:", result);
}
addSeriesToChart();
```

#### What Happens Inside?

* `JSON.stringify()` turns the native JS array/object into its textual representation—exactly what the `ChartSeriesGenerator` expects.
* The returned `result` typically contains:
  - A reference/id to the newly created series.
  - Any metadata the underlying chart engine returns (e.g., success flag, error messages).

If your chart framework exposes methods such as `.addSeries(series)` or similar, you’ll usually get back an object you can hand off there. If the generator already knows how to hook the series into the supplied layout, simply inspect `result.chartLayout` afterward to confirm integration.

---

### 3️⃣ Quick Test Snippet (Full Self‑Contained)

Here’s a minimal runnable example assuming the generator lives at `/api/chart-series-generator`. Replace the URL/path with wherever the real endpoint resides.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8"><title>Chart Series Demo</title></head>
<body>

<script src="/api/chart-series-generator"></script> <!-- loads the global `functions` -->

<script>
(async () => {
  const axisLabelsArray = ["January","February","March"];
  const dataPointsArray = [12,25,9];
  const defaultColor    = "#3498db";

  const chartLayoutObject = {
    title: "Monthly Sales",
    xAxis: { label: "Month" },
    yAxis: { label: "Units Sold" }
  };

  try {
    const res = await functions.ChartSeriesGenerator({
      labels: JSON.stringify(axisLabelsArray),
      data: JSON.stringify(dataPointsArray),
      color: defaultColor,
      chartLayout: JSON.stringify(chartLayoutObject)
    });

    console.log('✅ New series integrated:', res);

    /* OPTIONAL: Render the updated chart */
    // e.g., myChart.render(res.chartLayout); 
  } catch(err){
    console.error('❌ Failed to create series', err);
  }
})();
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

---

### 🎯 TL;DR

1. Build your plain JS arrays/objects (`axisLabelsArray`, `dataPointsArray`, `chartLayoutObject`).
2. Serialize each to a string with `JSON.stringify(...)`.
3. Pass those strings plus an optional hex colour to `functions.ChartSeriesGenerator({ … })`.
4. Use the returned payload to verify the series was attached to your chart layout.

Feel free to tweak the variable names or colours to match your own naming conventions!
